{% aside type="note" title="New interface coming soon" %}
This page shows the new SEGUI interface, which is in development. The current release
(v1.1.3) looks different, and features marked **In development** are not available in it yet.
{% /aside %}

Transpose locus alignments into per-sample sequence files. Locus alignments store one locus per file with a sequence for each sample. Transposing creates one file for each sample with a sequence for each locus, for example to build sample-level references for read mapping.

## Steps

1. Select `Sequences` in the navigation bar {% ref id="A" /%}.
2. Open `Transpose sequences` from the tool list on wide windows, or select the tool name {% ref id="B" /%} at the top of the page to switch tools.
3. Add the input files {% ref id="C" /%} with `Browse files`, `Browse folder`, or `Open archive`. SEGUI searches a folder and its subfolders for files that match the tool's input format. See [file input](/docs/gui-usage/intro/#file-input) for mobile options.
4. Select the input format (optional) and the data type.
5. Select `Browse` under `Select output directory` to choose the output folder. On phones and tablets, enter a `Directory name` instead; SEGUI saves it in the app's own folder.
6. Turn on `Set interleaved format` to write interleaved FASTA (optional).
7. Select the run button {% ref id="D" /%}, labeled `Transpose`, to start the task.

## Output

The app writes each sample to its own folder: `<sample>/<sample>.fas`. Each sequence is named after the locus it comes from, which is the input file name without its extension. Gaps (`-`) and missing data (`?`) are removed, so the output is always unaligned FASTA. Samples missing from a locus are skipped for that locus.
